{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'pwd && git branch --show-current && git rev-parse --short HEAD && git status --short && cat collab/1791870000000-reviewer-srvm-phase5a.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p5a\nsrvm-phase5a\n8df1d117\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-check-hand-numbers.clj\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-cljstyle-final.log\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-cljstyle.log\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-dart.log\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-derive.cljc.backup\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-first-numbers.log\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-first-numeric-run.log\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-g0-final.log\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-g0-green.log\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-g0.log\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-golden-hashes.log\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-hand-numbers.log\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-jvm-final.log\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-jvm-stack-extra.log\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-jvm.log\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-kondo-final.log\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-kondo.log\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-mutations-final.log\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-mutations-verified.log\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-mutations.clj\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-mutations.log\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-node-final.log\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-node-selected.log\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-node.log\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-restored-g0.log\n?? collab/1791850000000-5a-whitespace.log\n?? collab/1791850000000-vm-engineer-srvm-phase5a.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1791850000000-vm-engineer-srvm-phase5a.prompt.md\n?? collab/1791850000000-vm-engineer-srvm-phase5a.report.md\n?? collab/1791870000000-reviewer-derive-fresh.log\n?? collab/1791870000000-reviewer-derive.cljc.backup\n?? collab/1791870000000-reviewer-mutants-part1.log\n?? collab/1791870000000-reviewer-mutants-part2.log\n?? collab/1791870000000-reviewer-mutants.clj\n?? collab/1791870000000-reviewer-mutants.log\n?? collab/1791870000000-reviewer-srvm-phase5a.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md\n?? collab/1791870000000-reviewer-srvm-phase5a.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log\n?? collab/1791870000000-reviewer-srvm-phase5a.prompt.md\n?? collab/1791910000000-vm-engineer-srvm-phase5a-r2.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1791910000000-vm-engineer-srvm-phase5a-r2.prompt.md\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-11 06:39:41 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-11 13:39:41 Asia/Bangkok\n\n# Adversarial review of slice 5a (the resolved vector, addresses, A -> H)\n\nReviewer: claude-fable-5-1 (non-author family). Worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p5a`, branch `srvm-phase5a`, HEAD 8df1d117. Read-only review; the only files I created are under `collab/` (this file, my byte backup `1791870000000-reviewer-derive.cljc.backup`, the mutation script `1791870000000-reviewer-mutants.clj` and its logs). Nothing staged or committed. `git diff master...HEAD --name-only` lists exactly `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive.cljc` and `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc`; `git diff --quiet HEAD -- src/` passes now.\n\n## What I ran (all in this worktree, none backgrounded with `&`)\n\n- `clj -M:kondo --lint src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive.cljc test/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc`: errors 0, warnings 0.\n- Trailing-whitespace scan on both files: 0 lines.\n- `clj -M:cljs collab/1791870000000-reviewer-mutants.clj` (part 1, X01-X06, log `1791870000000-reviewer-mutants-part1.log`), then the same script with ids `X07 ... X24` (part 2, log `1791870000000-reviewer-mutants-part2.log`, exit 0). The script first recomputes all ten pinned checksums through `dc/image-hash`; then per mutant it rewrites `derive.cljc`, reloads it, runs EVERY `derive-test` var (so every killing test is named), and restores from my backup in `finally`, asserting `cmp` and `git diff --quiet HEAD -- src/` after each restore. I stopped part 1 at X07 because my first X07 variant looped forever (a queue read from the back re-reads a body that keeps enqueuing); I restored the source from the backup by hand (`cmp` and `git diff --quiet` verified), rewrote X07 and X20 as a one-time queue reversal, and ran part 2. The sandbox refused `clojure -M:cljs` and `clojure -M:test:cljs`; `clj -M:cljs` ran.\n- `clojure -M:test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.derive-test` in a fresh JVM on the restored source: 10 tests, 2946 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors (`1791870000000-reviewer-derive-fresh.log`). This clears an artifact in the script's in-JVM post-run pass, where `resolve-is-a-stage-value` showed 1 failure only because X21's leaked public var stays in the live namespace after `load-file` of the restored source; the same artifact adds a spurious `resolve-is-a-stage-value` entry to X22-X24's killer lists, each of which is killed by other named tests anyway.\n- NOT RUN by me: the wider JVM lanes (orchestrator: 35 tests / 3187 assertions green), Node (author only: 356 / 7067, with `Testing yin.vm.semantic-register.derive-test` present at line 31 of the author's node-final log), Dart (nobody yet), cljstyle (author only).\n\n## Findings (severity | file:line | evidence | correction)\n\n1. **MEDIUM | test/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive_test.cljc:235 and :300 | two emitter rules have no test that can fail.** Mutant X13 (`:ffi-call` pushes omitted, `derive.cljc:130`) and X14 (`:stream-put` operands swapped, `derive.cljc:132-133`) SURVIVED all ten tests. Why: §6.3 has no ffi or stream-put row, so `stack-goldens` never sees them; `dc/image-defect` does not check stack discipline; and the runtime corpus never executes either instruction to completion on both pipelines. B0 has no ffi and no `stream/put`; the register-corpus `:ffi-call` row throws on the unbound `x` before its `:ffi-call` runs, and `:streams` throws on the unbound `s` before its `:stream-put` runs, so old and new pipelines agree on `[:thrown ...]` whatever the emitter does after the throw; the respelled `:ffi`, `:streams` and r2 `:stream-put` rows of `C` are bytes-only. Result: a stack image that pops garbage for an FFI call, or puts the stream into the value and the value into the stream, would merge with every lane green. Correction (test-only, small): pin two more hand-derived goldens from §3.2, e.g. `{:op :op/echo :operands [(lit 1) (lit 2)]}` -> `[[:const 1] [:push] [:const 2] [:push] [:ffi-call :op/echo 2] [:halt]]` and `{:type :stream/put :target (lit :s) :val (lit 1)}` -> `[[:const :s] [:push] [:const 1] [:stream-put] [:halt]]`, in a new deftest (so `stack-goldens`' pinned count of 10 and its §6.3 meaning stay intact); and, since the oracle is the only relation to the old pipeline, add two runtime rows that reach a value on both pipelines: the respelled `:ffi` row under an explicit `{:op/echo (fn [a b] [a b])}` bridge, and a put onto a made stream `{:type :stream/put :target {:type :stream/make :buffer 4} :val (lit 1)}`. Confirm each new row kills X13/X14 before reporting.\n\n2. **LOW | derive_test.cljc:151-152 | composition departs from §6.1.3.** The design says the composition is `{:primitives vm/primitives ...}`, but `vm/primitives` (`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:375-420`) has no `list`, `vector` or `inc`, and the twelve rows §6.1.3 says \"run to a value\" include `:gensym` (`list`), `:store-ops` (`vector`) and `:define-then-call` (`list`, `inc`). The author extended both pipelines' registry identically with those three, which is the smallest reading that makes the design's 12/19/1 counts true; no throw row uses those names, so 19 is unaffected. This is a design-text defect, not an implementation one: §6.1.3 should name the three extra primitives (5b reuses the composition on the register kernel). Not blocking.\n\n3. **INFO | derive_test.cljc:330-331 | one non-vacuity assertion is vacuous as written.** `(not-any? #{[:free 'yin/def]} (derive/addresses (projected :define-then-call)))` cannot fail: A holds no `:var yin/def` (item 11 refuses it; the definition is a `:define` tuple), so `addresses(A)` can never contain it. The behaviour the design wanted guarded (the old-resolver leg skipping the definition operator) is in fact guarded by the four-way equality: mutant X19 (visit the operator) is KILLED by `lexical-address-law-stack` with 4 failures. Suggest pointing the assertion at `(derive/resolved-addresses (dr/resolve (vm/ast->datoms ast)))` so the intent is literal. Not blocking.\n\n4. **INFO | derive_test.cljc:51 | an unproven universal claim in a docstring.** `stack-corpus`'s docstring, carried over from `debruijn_linearize_test.cljc:83`, says \"Every named mnemonic `yin.vm.code/mnemonics` declares, reached at least once\"; nothing in this file asserts it, and `yin.vm.code` is the retired stack table (named in prose only; there is no require, so no hidden dependency). Reword to \"respelled from `debruijn_linearize_test`'s corpus\" or assert the coverage over the H images. Not blocking.\n\n5. **INFO | derive.cljc:81 | `'yin/def` hard-coded.** `resolved-addresses` compares the operator's `:yin.resolved/free` with the literal `'yin/def` where the resolver itself uses `vm/definition-operator` (`debruijn_resolve.cljc:104`). Same value today; a one-token change. Not blocking.\n\n6. **OPEN GATE | acceptance item 10.** Dart has not run anywhere (author: sandbox failure, honestly reported; orchestrator: lane busy). Everything I checked for Dart traps is clean (below), but the tri-host gate is still owed by the orchestrator before merge.\n\nNo defect found in `derive.cljc`'s derivations themselves: every emitter rule of §3.2, the chain construction of §1.2, the rewrite table of §1.3, the FIFO body order, the label pass and the side table match the design, and 22 of 24 mutants were killed by named tests.\n\n## Answers 1-6\n\n**1. Can each acceptance item's test fail?** Items 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9: yes, each has at least one mutant in the table below killed by exactly that named test (item 1: X04/X05/X06; item 2: X01-X04, X18; item 3: X21; item 4: X08-X12, X16, X23, X24; item 5: X07, X16, X17; item 6: determinism is structural, and X04/X05 error inside it, but no mutant can make a pure function non-deterministic, which is as the design expects; item 8: X01-X03, X07, X18-X20, X24; item 9: X22). Item 7: yes for the instructions the runtime rows reach (X08, X09, X11, X12, X15, X16 killed by the oracle alone or jointly), but NOT for `:ffi-call` operands and `:stream-put` (finding 1). Item 10: lane evidence, not a test. The author's 14-mutant table is consistent with my runs: my X04/X06 cover their M01-M04 territory, X08/X09 their M07/M08, X10-X12 their M05/M06/M11, X23 their M09, X07 their M14, X16 their M13. Their survivor M13 (`:return` -> `:halt`) is now killed; my X16 confirms the strengthened `stack-images-validate` kills the terminator swap with 130 failures. My two survivors are new and are not in their table because they never mutated the `:ffi-call` or `:stream-put` arms.\n\n**2. Goldens.** I derived rows 2, 3, 4, 5 (conditional operand: the push follows the join, Lelse = 6, Lend = 7), 6 (tail-call closure: main is six instructions, so Lbody = 6; the body's call carries `true` at pc 12), 7a, 7b, 8 and 9 by hand from the §3.2 table before running anything; all ten pinned vectors in `stack-golden-vectors` equal my derivations and equal the §6.3 text verbatim (I read the design's pc-by-pc listings against the pinned vectors one tuple at a time). All ten pinned checksum strings were recomputed through the public `dc/image-hash` in my script: 10 of 10 MATCH (part-1 and part-2 logs, lines 4-13). I also checked the `hand-derived-liveness` pins against §6.3's stated `L(p)` sets for all ten rows: identical.\n\n**3. Execution oracle.** Corpus membership: `C` = 26 B0 + 32 register + 15 respelled linearize-test rows + 4 `address-law-extra-fixtures` + 5 live fixtures + 12 r2 rows = 94, asserted; I compared the 15, 5 and 12 respelled rows with their sources (`debruijn_linearize_test.cljc:83-124`, `debruijn_register_compile_test.cljc:86, 618, 680, 717, 745, 997-1011`) and they are faithful. Counts 26/12/19/1 are asserted literally (`:301, :307-309`) and the value/throw/ping partition matches §6.1.3's named rows. `native-normalize` turns closures (host-typed or map) into `:closure`, continuations into `:continuation`, stream/cursor refs into `{:type :id}`, host fns into `:host-fn`, unwraps host-typed payloads, and recurses; applied to both sides and to the pinned `expected`. The `:op/ping` bridge is explicit and zero-argument; the expected observable `[true false :pong]` is asserted. Both pipelines compare `[halted? blocked? value]` and the normalized store. `dl/adapt` appears only in `stack-run :old` (`:148`); `stack-goldens` and `stack-determinism` call neither `dl/adapt` nor anything from the old lowerers, and nothing compares a new H with an old H. The one deviation is the composition (finding 2). Gap: finding 1.\n\n**4. Lexical-address law.** Four legs at `:324-326` in one `=`: `addresses(A)`, `addresses(resolve(A))`, `addresses(H)`, `resolved-addresses(dr/resolve(...))`. Note that the first two legs are the same computation (`addresses` on A calls `resolve` internally), so the real independence comes from the H leg and the old-resolver leg; the old-resolver leg is genuinely independent and is what kills X01-X03, X18-X20. Non-vacuity: `[:bound 1 _]` on `:nested-lambdas` and `[:bound 0 1]` on `:duplicate-param` are real; the `yin/def` one is vacuous as written (finding 3) but the behaviour is covered. `resolved-addresses` walks the resolver's tuples with the correct attribute names (`:yin/operator`, `:yin/operands`, `:yin/test`/`:yin/consequent`/`:yin/alternate`, `:yin/target`/`:yin/val-node`, `:yin/source`, `:yin/body`, `:yin/root`; checked against `debruijn_resolve.cljc:57-64, 331`), queues lambda bodies FIFO, and for a definition visits only operand 1; it uses `contains?` for the free attribute, so a nil-valued key cannot mislead it.\n\n**5. Scope and portability.** Only the two files of §10.1 are created; no existing file is touched. `derive.cljc` requires only `yin.vm.semantic-register.code` and `yin.vm.debruijn` (fewer than §1.1 lists, since the rest are 5b's); no `#'` anywhere in either file; no reference to `yin.vm.code` except the docstring of finding 4. Traps checked: no `for`; no unary minus (`(- (count t) 2)` is binary, integers only); no protocol; no EDN strings (the round-trip test reads `pr-str` output, which carries no whitespace before closers); maps start from `{}` and `assoc-in` on `{}`; `#?(:cljd nil :clj ...)` with `:cljd` first and catch clauses `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)`; nil-valued keys handled with `contains?`/`if-let` where they matter (`:owner nil` for main; `:yin.resolved/free`). `case` on keywords only. Nothing in the derivation iterates a map into emitted structure (the only map iteration is finding the single root entity). Remaining risk is only the unrun Dart lane.\n\n**6. Readings and claims.** (a) Composition (finding 2): the author chose the smallest test-only reading and reported it; I agree with the reading and ask for the design text to be corrected outside 5a. (b) Checksums: the author pins `dc/image-hash` strings (SHA-256 through the existing descriptor function) as §10.1 item 4 demands and adds no identity to the stage value; consistent with the owner's \"A is the only code identity\" since H strings here are checksums of a derived image, pinned in a test, not names. (c) The frozen §8.3 adapter step is superseded by §0.1 ruling 3; the implementation has no adapter. (d) `resolve-is-a-stage-value` audits names only on the JVM (by design); on Node and Dart the form is elided, which is what §12 item 2 prescribes. (e) The strengthened `stack-images-validate` reconstructs chains with the same `db/resolve-name` production uses, so its \"independent\" comment at `:265` is independent of `derive`, not of `resolve-name`; the old-resolver leg of the law supplies that independence. (f) Unproven claim: finding 4. (g) The `(testing label)` form at `:303` passes a string from B0; fine on all hosts.\n\n## Mutant table (every mutant run against all ten derive tests; \"KILLED by\" lists every test with failures or errors)\n\n| Id | Mutant (derive.cljc) | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| X01 | de Bruijn depth off by one | KILLED: lexical-address-law-stack 24F, resolution-is-resolve-names 4F, stack-execution-oracle 19F, stack-goldens 1F, stack-images-validate 94F/32E |\n| X02 | frames outer-first (shadowing order across frames) | KILLED: lexical-address-law-stack 5F, resolution-is-resolve-names 2F, stack-images-validate 14F |\n| X03 | duplicate parameters collapsed (rightmost-wins lost) | KILLED: lexical-address-law-stack 4F, resolution-is-resolve-names 2F, stack-images-validate 3F |\n| X04 | `:load-bound`/`:load-free` swapped | KILLED: 7 tests incl. resolve-rewrites-only-vars-and-closures 188F, stack-goldens 9F |\n| X05 | closure body-pc off by one in `resolve` | KILLED: resolve-rewrites-only-vars-and-closures 36F, stack-execution-oracle 21F, errors in 4 others |\n| X06 | `:names` recorded for free vars too | KILLED: resolve-rewrites-only-vars-and-closures 51F |\n| X07 | sibling bodies reversed (not FIFO) | KILLED: lexical-address-law-stack 3F, stack-images-validate 28F |\n| X08 | every label marked one pc late | KILLED: stack-execution-oracle 31F, stack-goldens 3F, stack-images-validate 94F |\n| X09 | conditional join label placed before the else arm | KILLED: stack-execution-oracle 2F, stack-goldens 2F |\n| X10 | operator push omitted (operands still pushed) | KILLED: stack-goldens 8F |\n| X11 | operator emitted after operands | KILLED: lexical-address-law-stack 24F, stack-execution-oracle 53F, stack-goldens 7F, stack-images-validate 6F |\n| X12 | tail flag forced true | KILLED: stack-execution-oracle 1F, stack-goldens 8F |\n| X13 | `:ffi-call` pushes omitted | **SURVIVED** (finding 1) |\n| X14 | `:stream-put` operands swapped | **SURVIVED** (finding 1) |\n| X15 | `:store-put` key/value swapped | KILLED: stack-execution-oracle 3F |\n| X16 | main/lambda terminators swapped | KILLED: stack-execution-oracle 1F, stack-goldens 10F, stack-images-validate 130F |\n| X17 | side-table `:source` shifted | KILLED: stack-images-validate 115F/24E |\n| X18 | `addresses` reads the wrong slice of `:load-bound` | KILLED: lexical-address-law-stack 25F, resolution-is-resolve-names 4F |\n| X19 | `resolved-addresses` visits a definition's operator | KILLED: lexical-address-law-stack 4F |\n| X20 | `resolved-addresses` bodies reversed | KILLED: lexical-address-law-stack 3F |\n| X21 | stage-value leak: a public `vector-digest` fn | KILLED: resolve-is-a-stage-value 1F (JVM audit only) |\n| X22 | `register-image` returns instead of throwing | KILLED: register-image-is-not-in-5a 1F |\n| X23 | `:define` emits the register instead of the name | KILLED: stack-execution-oracle 6F, stack-goldens 2F, stack-images-validate 4F |\n| X24 | `:resume` operand not walked | KILLED: lexical-address-law-stack 2F, stack-execution-oracle 2F, stack-goldens 1F |\n\n22 killed, 2 survived. Source restored byte for byte after every mutant (`cmp` and `git diff --quiet HEAD -- src/` asserted in the script; final state verified again by hand).\n\n## Judgement\n\nThe derivations are correct against the design and the ten goldens and checksums verify by independent derivation and recomputation. The slice is one small test addition away from merge: the two surviving mutants show that the `:ffi-call` push sequence and the `:stream-put` operand order, both real stack-kernel semantics, are currently unguarded by any test that can fail. That is the only blocking item; findings 2-5 are non-blocking notes, and the Dart lane remains the orchestrator's gate.\n\nVERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
